Xbox Game Pass reportedly lost millions of subscribers after Microsoft raised the service’s price in fall 2025. According to Xbox chief strategy officer Matthew Ball, the roughly 50% price increase, combined with the push to add Call of Duty as a day-one release, had a major impact on the subscription’s user base.

The exact number of remaining subscribers is still unclear, but the drop is significant considering Xbox previously announced 34 million Game Pass subscribers in 2024. Earlier this year, a leaked memo from new Xbox CEO Asha Sharma admitted the service had become too expensive for players and that Microsoft needed to offer a better value equation.

Microsoft has already started trying to correct course. In April, the company lowered Game Pass pricing again and removed new Call of Duty releases from the service’s day-one lineup, suggesting Xbox is still trying to figure out the right balance between price, blockbuster games, and subscriber retention. Do you think Game Pass became too expensive? 🎮


